<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The rest of this app's Info.plist is generated from the
	     INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings. What lives here is the three keys
	     that make the journal a folder the user owns rather than app storage
	     they cannot see — together they are what "the files are the journal"
	     (ADR 0001) looks like from the Files app — and, at the end, the
	     sentences the system says on Aujour's behalf when a Content Template
	     asks for the day's calendar, when the panel asks for the day's
	     photographs, and when a {{location}} widget asks where the device is.

	     The pair below publishes the app's own Documents folder under "On My
	     iPhone", which is where a journal starts when iCloud Drive is off
	     (ADR 0004). They are written out here rather than as build settings
	     because `INFOPLIST_KEY_UIFileSharingEnabled` is silently ignored —
	     not every Info.plist key has a build setting behind it, and a missing
	     one shows up as a folder nobody can find rather than as a build
	     error. -->
	<key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
	<true/>
	<key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- And this one publishes Aujour's iCloud Drive folder, the default
	     Journal Root: IsDocumentScopePublic puts the container's Documents
	     folder in the Files app under iCloud Drive › Aujour, and
	     SupportedFolderLevels = Any lets the Path Template nest entries in
	     YYYY/MM subfolders there.

	     Apple only re-reads this when the app's version changes, so a change
	     here needs MARKETING_VERSION or CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION bumped with
	     it, or the folder keeps its old visibility. -->
	<key>NSUbiquitousContainers</key>
	<dict>
		<key>iCloud.com.julesseguin.aujour</key>
		<dict>
			<key>NSUbiquitousContainerIsDocumentScopePublic</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSUbiquitousContainerName</key>
			<string>Aujour</string>
			<key>NSUbiquitousContainerSupportedFolderLevels</key>
			<string>Any</string>
		</dict>
	</dict>

	<!-- What the system alert says when {{events}} or {{reminders}} is in
	     somebody's Content Template. Written out here rather than as build
	     settings for the same reason as the file-sharing pair at the top: a
	     usage description that does not reach the bundle is a crash the
	     moment access is asked for, and there is no build error to see it
	     coming.

	     Full access, and it is the read half that is wanted: Aujour never
	     writes an event or a reminder — it copies the day's into the entry
	     being spawned, as markdown, and from then on they are words in the
	     user's file like any other. -->
	<key>NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aujour fills in {{events}} with the day's calendar events when it starts a new entry. Your calendar is only ever read, and what it says goes no further than the markdown file for that day.</string>
	<key>NSRemindersFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aujour fills in {{reminders}} with the day's reminders when it starts a new entry. Your reminders are only ever read, and what they say goes no further than the markdown file for that day.</string>

	<!-- And what it says when somebody asks the suggestions panel to show the
	     photographs from the day they are writing, or asks a {{location}}
	     widget where that day's photographs were taken. Here rather than as a
	     build setting for the same reason as the two above.

	     Two things ask for it and nothing else does: adding a photo with the
	     button above the keyboard goes through the system picker, which runs
	     in a process of its own and needs no permission at all. Saying no
	     costs the panel and the places, and nothing more.

	     The second sentence is here because reading where somebody's
	     photographs were taken is a meaningfully larger claim than showing
	     them the pictures, and this string is the only place the system ever
	     puts that claim in front of them. Leaving it to the panel's sentence
	     would be asking for one thing and doing two. -->
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aujour offers you the photos from the day you're writing about, so adding one is a single tap. It also reads where that day's photos were taken, to offer those places when you tap a {{location}} widget. Your library is only ever read, one day at a time, and nothing it finds goes anywhere but the markdown file for that day.</string>

	<!-- And what it says when somebody taps a {{location}} widget and asks
	     Aujour to find the place they are in. Here rather than as a build
	     setting for the same reason as the ones above.

	     When in use, and only ever once per question: the widget asks because
	     a finger landed on it and somebody is looking at the answer. There is
	     nothing Aujour would do with a location in the background, and saying
	     no costs the offer and nothing else — the place is typed instead. -->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aujour offers the place you're in when you tap a {{location}} widget, so you don't have to type it. It's only ever asked for while you're looking at the answer, and the place goes nowhere but the markdown file for that day.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
